Each die is shoreline-bound: four HBM stacks need edge, not area. That is why SRAM up to the floor is
nearly free, and why N3E would shrink only logic that does not set die size. Silicon cost is about
${{ U.die|n(0) }} per package ({{ U.dies_per_wafer|n(0) }} dies/wafer, Murphy yield {{ U.yield|pct }} at D0 = 0.10/cm²).
HBM and packaging dominate the BOM; NRE dominates unit cost ([04](04_results.md#cost)).

## 3. How a decode step flows

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant SEQ as Sequencer
  participant HBM as HBM stream
  participant CE as Compute
  participant RT as Router
  participant LNK as Links
  Note over HBM: static weights of layers l+1..l+k<br/>prefetched into staging while layer l runs
  SEQ->>CE: layer l attention chain (mHC, wqkv, wq_b, indexer, sparse attn, wo_a, wo_b)
  CE->>LNK: all-reduce #1 (6 x 5120 BF16), ~1.4 µs
  LNK->>CE: residual + mHC post
  CE->>RT: gate scores stream out of the MAC array
  RT->>HBM: expert ids (top-6 x 6 rows → ~35 distinct), 0.1 µs
  HBM->>CE: routed experts stream (dominant bytes) into the GEMV lanes
  CE->>LNK: all-reduce #2
  Note over CE,HBM: layer l+1 attention starts as soon as #2 lands;<br/>its weights are already staged
```

**The design rule** applies to MDA-1 and to GPU software alike. For one sequence, layer *l*'s expert
fetch cannot start until layer *l*'s attention chain and gate finish. The HBM stays busy only if that
chain finishes before the prefetcher runs out of *static* (data-independent) weights to stage. At TP4
that is about 55 MB per package per layer: 29 MB sharded attention, 9 MB shared expert, 17 MB
replicated projections. That is about 9 µs at the calibrated 6.2 TB/s.

| chain per layer | time | HBM busy |
|---|---|---|
| MDA-1: ~13 ops x 0.2 µs + 2 x 1.4 µs all-reduce + router + first byte | ~6 µs | {{ C.asic.hbm_busy|pct }} |
| GPU dataflow-style software: ~13 x 0.5 µs hand-offs + 2 x 1.4 µs + router | ~10 µs | {{ C.gpu_df_tp4.hbm_busy|pct }} |
| GPU today: ~9 dependent kernel stages x {{ cal.kernel_overhead_us|n(1) }} µs + collectives | ~{{ (K.fixed4_ms*1000/43)|n(0) }} µs | {{ C.gpu_tp4.hbm_busy|pct }} |

Both of the first two keep the HBM nearly saturated. That is why they reach the same speed. The
budget shrinks as packages are added, because each package's static bytes per layer shrink. That is
where MDA-1's shorter hand-off starts to show ([04](04_results.md#strong-scaling)).

## 4. Datapaths and number formats

* **FP4 x FP8 GEMV lanes.** Expert weights arrive as E2M1 nibbles with one E8M0 exponent per 32 elements. The lane multiplies by the FP8 E4M3 activation, accumulates 32 products in FP32, then applies the block scale. E8M0 is a power of two, so the scale is an exponent add on the partial sum, not a multiply. The same lane takes FP8 x FP8 for dense projections (E8M0 per 32x32 block).
* **Engine shape.** 16 rows (GEMM M) x 1,024 columns per engine. Decode presents M = 6 (verify rows); at batch 1 that is about 1 row per routed expert. SCALE-Sim shows a 128x128 weight-stationary array at 0.3-1.5% utilization on these shapes, and the 16-row engine at 3-21% ([04](04_results.md#scale-sim)). Batch-1 decode needs only ~0.2 PF effective, so utilization binds only at batch ≥ 16.
* **Sparse-attention unit.** Gathers 128 SWA entries (FP8) and 512 top-k latent entries (FP4 + E4M3/16) per row, dequantizes on the fly, and runs 64-head QK/PV with the attention sink. One gather serves all 64 heads (536 FLOP/B, 01 §3).
* **Top-k units.** Streaming insertion networks for indexer top-512 (up to 1M scores at layer 20) and the 2,048-block candidate pool.

## 5. MoE router in hardware (verified RTL)

`rtl/moe_topk_router.sv` has 8 lanes of 6-entry insertion queues. They absorb 8 gate keys per cycle
(48 beats for 384 experts), then a 48-entry merge pass produces the result. The selection key is
sqrt-softplus(logit) plus the `noaux_tc` bias; ties go to the lower expert id.
**{{ RTL.match }} tokens match a numpy golden model in Icarus Verilog; latency {{ RTL.latency_cycles }} cycles
({{ (RTL.latency_cycles/1.5)|n(0) }} ns at 1.5 GHz).** In the tornado, router latency from 0.05 to 2 µs moves
speed by under 1%: the prefetcher hides it. **A hardware router is not where silicon earns its keep.**

